    "q-143832": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143832",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X+Y)].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(X+Y)] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+0)\\cdot f(0,0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & \\,+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)\\cdot f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\cdot f(0,0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & \\,+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\cdot f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+0\\cdot 0.25+0\\cdot 0.05+(-1)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5+0+0-0.2 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
