    "q-143830": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143830",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\sqrt{X}] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\sqrt{1})\\cdot f(1,1)+(2\\cdot \\sqrt{1})\\cdot f(1,2)+(1\\cdot \\sqrt{4})\\cdot f(4,1)+(2\\cdot \\sqrt{4})\\cdot f(4,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot f(1,1)+2\\cdot f(1,2)+2\\cdot f(4,1)+4\\cdot f(4,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.1+1+0.4+0.8 \\\\  & =2.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
