    "q-111896": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111896",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given in the table below. Find $\\text{E}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(X+Y)].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=\\pi $ | $0.4$ | $0$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with supports ${S}_{X}$ and ${S}_{Y},$ respectively, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability mass function with support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}.$ If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+0)\\cdot f(0,0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & \\,+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi +0)\\cdot f(\\pi ,0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\cdot f(0,0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & \\,+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\cdot f(\\pi ,0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\cdot f(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.5+0\\cdot 0.4+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0+0.5+0+0 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
