    "q-143886": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143886",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[Y+1],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=y+1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y+1] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }(y+1){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }(y+1)[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{4}{\\pi }(y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{32}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
