    "q-143880": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143880",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{3}(y+1).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4y(y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{3}+8)-(\\frac{4}{3}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
