    "q-143904": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143904",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5},\\, & 2-y\\le x\\le \\frac{y+4}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2-y\\le x\\le \\frac{y+4}{2}\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14270831/assets/9dce6bf320cb848a36da0592645c0aca40b3c310a5f1394d8f7a0423ddd0ede6.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{x}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{(y+4)/2}\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{x}{5}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2-y}^{(y+4)/2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[x]_{x=2-y}^{x=(y+4)/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{y+4}{2}-(2-y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
