    "q-143863": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143863",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{XY}{3}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-3x; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-3x\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14270831/assets/b5a9355f0c2a39a1da71f3132c1eb3d7c72464af62b23f8a26d76df306bf9773.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{XY}{3}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}\\frac{xy}{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-3x}\\frac{xy}{3}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-3x}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(3-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =3[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
