    "q-143834": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143834",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[XY],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3xy},\\, & 1\\le x\\le y,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le y,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{2}\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14270831/assets/09bf58b8505f860709d705f128b2bafbd6916382de56ba66d26a1a56319f0141.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{y}xy\\cdot \\frac{2}{3xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}[x]_{x=1}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}(y-1)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-y]}_{y=e}^{y={e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[(\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}-{e}^{2})-(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-e)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{e}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2}+e) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}}{3}-{e}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-143834-a",
