    "q-143850": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143850",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[4(X-1)],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=4(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14270831/assets/bd8d2d36a0fe570e6ccdb096c6c39bc9c9edeed9b008dada504801025055438f.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4(X-1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}4(x-1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(x-1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}4(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1){\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)[y]_{y=1}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)({x}^{2}-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =3[(4-\\frac{8}{3}-2+2)-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+1)] \\\\  & =3(\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
